# Copy this file to application-test.properties (which is git-ignored) and fill in the Vault
# values, then run the console integration tests:
#
#   ./gradlew :datahub-console:integrationTest
#
# The `test` profile is activated by that task. Without this file Spring cannot resolve
# vault.address, so VaultConfigurationLoader fails at environment-prepared time and every
# @SpringBootTest in the module dies with "Cannot invoke String.trim() because address is null"
# before its first assertion runs.
#
# Everything else the tests need (the datahub.url they call, the OAuth2 client registration)
# comes from Vault itself, so these three values are the whole of it.

# Vault. The AppRole secret-id is read from the VAULT_SECRET-ID environment variable (set it in
# your shell or run configuration), NOT this file, so it never lands in source control.
vault.address=< vault url here >
vault.role-id=<your-vault-approle-role-id>
# Client certificate for a Vault listener that requires mutual TLS (PKCS12, or JKS by the .jks
# extension), and optionally the CA that signed Vault's server certificate. Leave unset for a
# Vault that does not require a client certificate. Also settable as VAULT_KEYSTORE,
# VAULT_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD, VAULT_TRUSTSTORE and VAULT_TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD.
#vault.keystore=< path to client .p12 here >
#vault.keystore-password=< keystore password here >
#vault.truststore=< path to CA .p12 here >
#vault.truststore-password=< truststore password here >

# Anthropic key for AnthropicLlmClientIT, the one test that spends money. Optional: the test is
# skipped with a reason when neither this nor the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY environment variable is set.
#datahub.chat.api-key=sk-ant-...
